1
00:00:03,159 --> 00:00:06,659
chapter 4.

2
00:00:09,940 --> 00:00:13,120
I had thus learned a second fact of

3
00:00:13,120 --> 00:00:14,860
great importance:

4
00:00:14,860 --> 00:00:17,139
this was that the planet the little

5
00:00:17,139 --> 00:00:19,839
prince came from was scarcely larger

6
00:00:19,839 --> 00:00:22,659
than a house!

7
00:00:22,659 --> 00:00:25,240
But that did not really surprise me much.

8
00:00:25,240 --> 00:00:27,159
I knew very well that in addition to the

9
00:00:27,159 --> 00:00:29,500
great planets-- such as Earth, Jupiter, Mars

10
00:00:29,500 --> 00:00:31,320
and Venus which have been given names,

11
00:00:31,320 --> 00:00:34,179
there are also hundreds of others which

12
00:00:34,179 --> 00:00:36,519
are often so small that it is difficult

13
00:00:36,519 --> 00:00:38,919
to see them through a telescope.

14
00:00:38,919 --> 00:00:40,899
When an astronomer discovers one of

15
00:00:40,899 --> 00:00:43,778
these he does not give it a name, but

16
00:00:43,778 --> 00:00:45,339
only a number.

17
00:00:45,339 --> 00:00:48,159
He might call it, for example, "Asteroid

18
00:00:48,159 --> 00:00:50,379
325.

19
00:00:50,379 --> 00:00:52,120
giving such a number to the newly

20
00:00:52,120 --> 00:00:54,039
discovered planet or an asteroid makes

21
00:00:54,039 --> 00:00:56,198
it very easy for the grown-ups to accept

22
00:00:56,198 --> 00:00:58,659
the fact

23
00:00:58,659 --> 00:01:00,278
I have serious reason to believe that

24
00:01:00,278 --> 00:01:01,659
the planet from which the little prince

25
00:01:01,659 --> 00:01:07,059
came is the asteroid known as B--612

26
00:01:07,179 --> 00:01:09,399
the asteroid is so small that it cannot

27
00:01:09,399 --> 00:01:12,100
be seen with a naked eye that is why it

28
00:01:12,100 --> 00:01:14,919
has to be observed through a telescope.

29
00:01:14,919 --> 00:01:17,799
That observation was by a Turkish

30
00:01:17,799 --> 00:01:21,820
astronomer, in 1909..

31
00:01:21,820 --> 00:01:24,698
On making his discovery, the astronomer

32
00:01:24,698 --> 00:01:26,220
had presented it to the International

33
00:01:26,220 --> 00:01:28,958
Astronomical Congress, in a great

34
00:01:28,958 --> 00:01:30,519
demonstration.

35
00:01:30,519 --> 00:01:32,440
But because of his Turkish costume

36
00:01:32,440 --> 00:01:35,980
nobody had believed what he said.

37
00:01:35,980 --> 00:01:39,039
Grown-ups are like that...

38
00:01:39,039 --> 00:01:41,379
Fortunately, however, for the reputation

39
00:01:41,379 --> 00:01:45,278
of Asteroid B--612, a Turkish dictator made

40
00:01:45,278 --> 00:01:47,320
a law that his subjects, under pain of

41
00:01:47,320 --> 00:01:51,278
death, should change to European costume.

42
00:01:51,278 --> 00:01:55,000
So in 1920 the astronomer gave his

43
00:01:55,000 --> 00:01:58,240
demonstration all over again, dressed in

44
00:01:58,240 --> 00:02:01,360
style in an elegant suit

45
00:02:01,360 --> 00:02:05,198
And this time everybody accepted his

46
00:02:05,198 --> 00:02:07,360
report.

47
00:02:07,360 --> 00:02:09,220
If I have told you these details about

48
00:02:09,220 --> 00:02:12,578
asteroid b612 and made a note of its

49
00:02:12,578 --> 00:02:13,778
number to you,

50
00:02:13,778 --> 00:02:16,179
it is on account of the grown-ups and

51
00:02:16,179 --> 00:02:17,620
their ways

52
00:02:17,620 --> 00:02:20,500
grown-ups love figures When you tell

53
00:02:20,500 --> 00:02:21,820
them that you have made a new friend,

54
00:02:21,820 --> 00:02:23,919
they never ask you any questions about

55
00:02:23,919 --> 00:02:26,679
essential matters. They never say to you,

56
00:02:26,679 --> 00:02:29,980
"What does his voice sound like? What

57
00:02:29,980 --> 00:02:32,019
games does he love best?

58
00:02:32,019 --> 00:02:34,480
Does he collect butterflies?"

59
00:02:34,480 --> 00:02:36,459
Instead, they demand:

60
00:02:36,459 --> 00:02:39,160
"How old is he? How many brothers does he

61
00:02:39,160 --> 00:02:39,880
have

62
00:02:39,880 --> 00:02:41,919
How much does he weigh? How much money

63
00:02:41,919 --> 00:02:44,019
does his father make?"

64
00:02:44,019 --> 00:02:46,000
Only from these figures do they think

65
00:02:46,000 --> 00:02:48,578
they have learned anything about him.

66
00:02:48,578 --> 00:02:51,099
If you were to say to the grown-ups

67
00:02:51,099 --> 00:02:53,259
I've seen a beautiful house made of

68
00:02:53,259 --> 00:02:55,058
Rosie brick, with geraniums on the

69
00:02:55,058 --> 00:02:57,640
windowsills and doves on the roof,"

70
00:02:57,640 --> 00:02:59,679
they would not be able to get any idea

71
00:02:59,679 --> 00:03:01,179
of such a house

72
00:03:01,179 --> 00:03:03,819
You would have to say to them: "I saw a

73
00:03:03,819 --> 00:03:06,700
house that cost four thousand pounds

74
00:03:06,700 --> 00:03:10,599
Then they would exclaim: "Oh, what a pretty

75
00:03:10,599 --> 00:03:12,640
house that is

76
00:03:12,640 --> 00:03:14,798
thus if you say to them:

77
00:03:14,798 --> 00:03:16,960
"The proof that the little prince existed

78
00:03:16,960 --> 00:03:20,259
is that he was charming but he laughed,

79
00:03:20,259 --> 00:03:22,900
and that he was looking for his sheep

80
00:03:22,900 --> 00:03:25,720
now If anybody wants a sheep it proves

81
00:03:25,720 --> 00:03:27,519
that he exists."

82
00:03:27,519 --> 00:03:29,200
And what good would it do to tell them

83
00:03:29,200 --> 00:03:30,160
that?

84
00:03:30,160 --> 00:03:31,960
They would shrug their shoulders, and

85
00:03:31,960 --> 00:03:33,940
treat you as if you were a child.

86
00:03:33,940 --> 00:03:36,579
But if you said to them: "The planet he

87
00:03:36,579 --> 00:03:39,940
came from was Asteroid B--612,"

88
00:03:39,940 --> 00:03:42,460
then they would be convinced, and leave

89
00:03:42,460 --> 00:03:45,400
you in peace from their questions.

90
00:03:45,400 --> 00:03:47,558
They are like that.

91
00:03:47,558 --> 00:03:50,079
One must not hold it against them.

92
00:03:50,079 --> 00:03:51,819
Children should always show great

93
00:03:51,819 --> 00:03:55,839
forbearance towards grown-up people.

94
00:03:55,839 --> 00:03:57,579
But certainly, for those of us who

95
00:03:57,579 --> 00:03:59,319
understand life

96
00:03:59,319 --> 00:04:02,740
we could not care less about figures

97
00:04:02,740 --> 00:04:04,660
I should have liked to start this story

98
00:04:04,660 --> 00:04:07,298
like a fairy tale I should have liked to

99
00:04:07,298 --> 00:04:08,200
say:

100
00:04:08,200 --> 00:04:10,359
"Once upon a time there was a little

101
00:04:10,359 --> 00:04:13,240
prince who lived on a planet scarcely

102
00:04:13,240 --> 00:04:16,000
bigger than himself, and who had need of

103
00:04:16,000 --> 00:04:18,480
a friend

104
00:04:18,579 --> 00:04:21,160
To those who understand life,

105
00:04:21,160 --> 00:04:22,839
that would have given a much greater

106
00:04:22,839 --> 00:04:26,579
error of truth to my story.

107
00:04:26,619 --> 00:04:29,019
For I do not want anyone to read my book

108
00:04:29,019 --> 00:04:31,000
